Fix hang when command lines split \r\n across 224-byte read buffer boundary
(issue #40; upstream bug, unfixed in v1.13 and on master).

When a command line splits \r\n across the LINE_BUF_SIZE (224-byte) boundary
(for instance, 223 bytes of command plus \r\n), scan_line_end() does not detect
the endline because memchr(s, '\r', size - 1) only inspects up to index 222
when size == 224. Upon reading 224 bytes without finding an endline,
conn_process_io() in STATE_WANT_COMMAND transitions to STATE_WANT_ENDLINE and
unconditionally resets c->cmd_read = 0, discarding the \r at index 223.

When the next read() arrives in STATE_WANT_ENDLINE, \n is placed at index 0.
scan_line_end() fails to find \r\n because \r was discarded, and the daemon
hangs indefinitely waiting for a line terminator that the client already sent.
Subsequent commands on the connection are swallowed as the missing terminator,
desynchronizing the protocol stream. The same boundary discard occurs if an
oversized line splits \r\n across subsequent 224-byte boundaries in
STATE_WANT_ENDLINE.

Additionally, scan_line_end() previously returned 0 immediately if the first
\r found was not followed by \n, ignoring any subsequent \r\n sequences in the
buffer.

Fix this by:
1. Looping in scan_line_end() over all occurrences of \r in the buffer.
2. When c->cmd_read reaches LINE_BUF_SIZE without finding an endline in
   STATE_WANT_COMMAND or STATE_WANT_ENDLINE, check whether the final byte in
   the buffer is \r. If so, preserve it at index 0 (c->cmd[0] = '\r';
   c->cmd_read = 1;) across the transition to or continuation of
   STATE_WANT_ENDLINE so the subsequent read can match the incoming \n.

--- a/prot.c
+++ b/prot.c
@@ -761,15 +761,16 @@
 static size_t
 scan_line_end(const char *s, int size)
 {
-    char *match;
+    const char *match, *cur = s;
+    int rem = size - 1;
 
-    match = memchr(s, '\r', size - 1);
-    if (!match)
-        return 0;
+    while (rem > 0 && (match = memchr(cur, '\r', rem))) {
+        if (match[1] == '\n')
+            return match - s + 2;
 
-    /* this is safe because we only scan size - 1 chars above */
-    if (match[1] == '\n')
-        return match - s + 2;
+        rem -= (match - cur + 1);
+        cur = match + 1;
+    }
 
     return 0;
 }
@@ -1951,8 +1952,13 @@
             // Command line too long.
             // Put connection into special state that discards
             // the command line until the end line is found.
-            c->cmd_read = 0; // discard the input so far
             c->state = STATE_WANT_ENDLINE;
+            if (c->cmd[LINE_BUF_SIZE - 1] == '\r') {
+                c->cmd[0] = '\r';
+                c->cmd_read = 1;
+            } else {
+                c->cmd_read = 0; // discard the input so far
+            }
         }
         // We have an incomplete line, so just keep waiting.
         return;
@@ -1981,7 +1987,12 @@
 
         if (c->cmd_read == LINE_BUF_SIZE) {
             // Keep discarding the input since no EOL was found.
-            c->cmd_read = 0;
+            if (c->cmd[LINE_BUF_SIZE - 1] == '\r') {
+                c->cmd[0] = '\r';
+                c->cmd_read = 1;
+            } else {
+                c->cmd_read = 0;
+            }
         }
         return;
 
